            <ArticleTitle>MR findings in athletes with pubalgia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>270-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To describe the MR findings in athletes with pubalgia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN AND PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Pelvic MR images of 32 athletes (30 men, 2 women) with pubalgia were studied. T1-weighted and T2-weighted (SE and FSE) and STIR images in the axial and coronal planes were obtained on a 1.5-T system. Images were reviewed for general pelvic pathology. Special attention was given to the pubic symphysis, groin and pelvic musculature, and to the abdominal wall musculature.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Thirty surgically confirmed cases comprise the study group. Abnormalities in the following were found: pubic symphysis (21/30), abdominal wall (27/30), groin musculature, including rectus abdominis (21/30), pectineus (6/30), and adductor muscle group (18/30).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Pubalgia is a complex process which is frequently multifactorial. The MRI findings can alter the surgical approach.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
